    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The brittle-ductile transition is a depth interval in the earth’s crust where the constitutive behavior of rock undergoes significant changes. The juxtaposition of brittle and ductile structures in the Bear Creek field area provides a unique opportunity to investigate the relationship between stress perturbations and deformation mechanisms in the brittle-ductile transition. We focus on outcrop SG10, which features an aplite dike offset through a contractional right step between two left-lateral strike-slip faults. Within the step, the dike undergoes substantial thinning and dike-parallel foliation develops within the surrounding granodiorite. Thin-section analysis reveals that quartz and biotite undergo crystal-plastic deformation, while feldspars deform brittlely. To test a variety of constitutive laws that might govern this style of deformation, we use the commercial finite element code, Abaqus/CAE. The model geometry is motivated by our kinematic model for the evolution of outcrop SG10, and the boundary conditions are consistent with previous geobarometry findings. We experiment with four constitutive models: linear elasticity, Mohr Coulomb elastoplasticity, Drucker Prager elastoplasticity, and Drucker Prager/Cap elastoplasticity. The model results for Drucker Prager/Cap elastoplasticity are most consistent with field and microstructural observations, which suggests that the constitutive behavior is pressure-dependent. Future analysis will experiment with time-dependent constitutive models (e.g., creep laws). In addition, electron backscatter diffraction (EBSD) analysis will identify the active deformation mechanisms at the crystallographic scale, which will better constrain the conditions of deformation and will provide a more rigorous means to assess the model results. The conclusions of this study will hopefully eliminate potential constitutive laws for brittle-ductile deformation, and will shed light on how granitic rocks respond to stress in the brittle-ductile transition.",
